# Run ASH as a pre-commit hook

ASH's repository defines hooks for the [pre-commit](https://pre-commit.com/) framework.
This page sets up the one that works, says what it scans, and what it costs per commit.

Prerequisites:

- pre-commit, running on Python 3.10 or later, or a `language_version` as shown
  [below](#python-version).
- A git repository with a `.pre-commit-config.yaml`.
- Network access to GitHub and PyPI the first time the hook runs.

## Add the hook {#add}

<!-- doctest: config kind=pre-commit -->
```yaml
repos:
  - repo: https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper
    rev: v{{VERSION}}
    hooks:
      - id: ash
        args: [--exclude-scanners, cdk-nag, --exclude-scanners, npm-audit]
```

Then install the git hook and run it once on every file:

<!-- doctest: illustrative reason="installs the hook environment from GitHub" -->
```bash
pre-commit install
pre-commit run ash --all-files
```

The first run builds the hook's environment and installs bandit and checkov, so it takes
longer than later runs. When the hook finds something, pre-commit prints `Failed` with
ASH's summary and `- exit code: 2`, and the commit stops. Exit code 1 means a scanner did not
run.

Ignore ASH's output directory in git, so the reports are not committed and ASH does not scan
its own previous output. `ash config init` writes a `.ash/.gitignore` that does this, or add
the line yourself:

```text
.ash/ash_output/
```

Do not ignore all of `.ash/`: ASH reads its configuration from `.ash/.ash.yaml`.

## What the hook runs {#behavior}

The `ash` hook runs `ashv3 --mode=precommit` with your `args`. Precommit mode:

- runs five scanners: bandit, detect-secrets, checkov, cdk-nag and npm-audit. `--scanners`
  can add others but cannot remove these; `--exclude-scanners` removes them.
- scans the whole repository on every run, not only the staged files. A commit that touches
  one file is still blocked by a finding in a file committed earlier.
- prints simplified output and writes the usual reports to `.ash/ash_output/`.

The `args` above leave out cdk-nag and npm-audit, because the stock hook cannot run them:

- cdk-nag needs ASH's `cdk` extra, which the hook's environment does not install, and
  Node.js.
- npm-audit needs npm on `PATH`.

Without those `args`, ASH reports both scanners MISSING and the hook fails with exit code 1
on every commit, whatever the code contains. `ash-simple-scan` is the same hook under
another name.

### Run all five scanners {#all-scanners}

With Node.js and npm on your `PATH`, give the hook environment the `cdk` extra's
packages instead of excluding the scanners:

<!-- doctest: config kind=pre-commit -->
```yaml
repos:
  - repo: https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper
    rev: v{{VERSION}}
    hooks:
      - id: ash
        additional_dependencies:
          - "aws-cdk-lib>=2.273.0,<3.0.0"
          - "cdk-nag>=3.0,<4.0.0"
          - "constructs>=10.8,<11.0.0"
```

These are the `cdk` extra in ASH's
[pyproject.toml](https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper/blob/main/pyproject.toml);
check them against that file at the release you pin when you change `rev`. The environment
is about three times the size, and the first run takes longer.

Do not use `--no-fail-on-incomplete-scanners` to get past the MISSING scanners. With it, a
commit passes while two of the five scanners never ran, and the output says only `Passed`.

## When the hook runs {#stages}

The hook has no `stages` setting, so it runs on every commit. To run it less often, set one:

<!-- doctest: config kind=pre-commit -->
```yaml
repos:
  - repo: https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper
    rev: v{{VERSION}}
    hooks:
      - id: ash
        args: [--exclude-scanners, cdk-nag, --exclude-scanners, npm-audit]
        stages: [pre-push]
```

With `stages: [pre-push]`, install that hook type with
`pre-commit install --hook-type pre-push`, and a failing scan blocks `git push`. With
`stages: [manual]`, the hook runs only when you ask:
`pre-commit run --hook-stage manual ash`.

## What it costs {#cost}

Measured on a repository of four files:

| Run | Time |
|---|---|
| First run, with the hook environment and scanner installs | about 50 seconds |
| Later runs | 15 to 20 seconds |
| First run with the `cdk` packages | about 60 seconds |

Most of a later run passes before the first scanner starts, while ASH checks its scanner
installs; checkov is the slowest scanner. Scan time grows with the size of the repository, because every run
scans all of it. The hook environment and bandit and checkov take about 700 MB under
`~/.cache/pre-commit` and `~/.local/share/uv`, and the `cdk` packages add about 400 MB.

## Use an ASH you already installed {#local-hook}

If ASH is installed with `uv tool install` or in a virtual environment outside the
repository, a local hook runs that installation instead of building one per repository:

<!-- doctest: config kind=pre-commit -->
```yaml
repos:
  - repo: local
    hooks:
      - id: ash
        name: ASH
        entry: ash --mode precommit
        language: system
        pass_filenames: false
```

`ash` and `uv` must both be on `PATH` when git runs the hook: ASH installs bandit and checkov
with `uv`, and reports them MISSING when it cannot find it. `pass_filenames: false` is
required. Without it, pre-commit appends the staged file names, ASH reads the first one as a
command name and exits 2, the same code as a finding. This hook uses the extras and tools of
that installation, so cdk-nag runs if it has the `cdk` extra and Node.js is on `PATH`.

## Pre-commit on Python 3.9 {#python-version}

If pre-commit itself runs on Python 3.9, building the hook environment fails with
`No matching distribution found for hatchling`. Point the hook at a newer Python that is on
your `PATH`:

<!-- doctest: config kind=pre-commit -->
```yaml
repos:
  - repo: https://github.com/awslabs/automated-security-helper
    rev: v{{VERSION}}
    hooks:
      - id: ash
        language_version: python3.12
        args: [--exclude-scanners, cdk-nag, --exclude-scanners, npm-audit]
```

`default_language_version` at the top of the file does the same for every Python hook.

## A frozen rev is flagged as a secret {#frozen-rev}

`pre-commit autoupdate --freeze` replaces the tag with a 40-character commit SHA, and
detect-secrets reports that line of `.pre-commit-config.yaml` as a high-entropy string,
which fails the hook. Suppress that finding for that file, with the reason:

<!-- doctest: config kind=ash -->
```yaml
global_settings:
  suppressions:
    - path: .pre-commit-config.yaml
      rule_id: SECRET-HEX-HIGH-ENTROPY-STRING
      reason: pre-commit autoupdate --freeze pins hooks to commit SHAs
```

Put it in `.ash/.ash.yaml`. [Suppress a finding](suppress-findings.md) covers expiry dates
and narrower matches.

## The ash-full-scan hook {#full-scan}

`.pre-commit-hooks.yaml` also defines `ash-full-scan`, described as a container-mode scan.
It cannot run: it is a `language: script` hook, so pre-commit looks for an `ashv3` file in
the root of ASH's repository, finds none, and fails with ``Executable `.../ashv3` not
found`` before ASH starts. For a full scan before you push, run
`ash scan --mode container` yourself; see [Run ASH in a container](run-in-a-container.md).
